<section xml:id="sec-dot-product">
  <title>Dot Products and Orthogonality</title>

  <objectives>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Understand the relationship between the dot product, length, and distance.</p></li>
      <li><p>Understand the relationship between the dot product and orthogonality.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Vocabulary words:</em> <term>dot product</term>, <term>length</term>, <term>distance</term>, <term>unit vector</term>, <term>unit vector in the direction of <m>x</m></term>.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Essential vocabulary word:</em> <term>orthogonal</term>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this chapter, it will be necessary to find the <em>closest</em> point on a subspace to a given point, like so:
      </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[myxyz, thin border nodes]
  \coordinate (u) at (0,1,0);
  \coordinate (v) at (1.1,0,-.2);
  \coordinate (uxv) at (.2,0,1.1);
  \coordinate (x) at ($-1.1*(u)+(v)+1.5*(uxv)$);
  \begin{scope}[x=(u),y=(v),transformxy]
    \fill[seq-violet!30] (-2,-2) rectangle (2,2);
    \draw[seq-violet, help lines] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
  \end{scope}
  \point[seq-blue, "closest point" {below,text=seq-blue}] (y) at ($-1.1*(u)+1*(v)$);
  \coordinate (yu) at ($(y)+(u)$);
  \coordinate (yv) at ($(y)+(v)$);
  \pic[draw, right angle len=3mm] {right angle=(x)--(y)--(yu)};
  \pic[draw, right angle len=3mm] {right angle=(x)--(y)--(yv)};
  \point[seq-red, "$x$" {above,text=seq-red}] (xx) at (x);
  \draw[vector, thin] (y) -- (xx);
  \point at (0,0,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
</image>
<p>
      The closest point has the property that the difference between the two points is <em>orthogonal</em>, or <em>perpendicular</em>, to the subspace.  For this reason, we need to develop notions of orthogonality, length, and distance.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-the-dot-product">
    <title>The Dot Product</title>

    <p>
      The basic construction in this section is the <em>dot product</em>, which measures angles between vectors and computes the length of a vector.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <idx><h>Dot product</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <notation><usage><m>x\cdot y</m></usage><description>Dot product of two vectors</description></notation>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>dot product</term> of two vectors <m>x,y</m> in <m>\R^n</m> is
          <md>
<mrow>x\cdot y = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
\vdots \\
x_n
\end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix}
y_1 \\
y_2 \\
\vdots \\
y_n
\end{pmatrix}
            \;=\; x_1y_1 + x_2y_2 + \cdots + x_ny_n</mrow>
</md>.
          Thinking of <m>x,y</m> as column vectors, this is the same as <m>x^Ty</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      For example,
      <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
2 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
5 \\
6
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 2 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
5 \\
6
\end{pmatrix}
        = 1\cdot 4 + 2\cdot 5 + 3\cdot 6 = 32</mrow>
</md>.
      Notice that the dot product of two <em>vectors</em> is a <em>scalar</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can do arithmetic with dot products mostly as usual, as long as you remember you can only dot two vectors together, and that the result is a scalar.
    </p>

    <note>
      <title>Properties of the Dot Product</title>
      <idx><h>Dot product</h><h>properties of</h></idx>
      <p>
        Let <m>x,y,z</m> be vectors in <m>\R^n</m> and let <m>c</m> be a scalar.
        <ol>
          <li><p>
            <em>Commutativity:</em> <m>x\cdot y = y \cdot x</m>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <em>Distributivity with addition:</em> <m>(x + y)\cdot z = x\cdot z+y\cdot z</m>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <em>Distributivity with scalar multiplication:</em> <m>(cx)\cdot y = c(x\cdot y)</m>.
          </p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
      The dot product of a vector with itself is an important special case:
      <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
\vdots \\
x_n
\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
\vdots \\
x_n
\end{pmatrix}
        = x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \cdots + x_n^2</mrow>
</md>.
      Therefore, for any vector <m>x</m>, we have:
      <ul>
        <li><p><m>x\cdot x \geq 0</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>x\cdot x = 0 \iff x = 0.</m></p></li>
      </ul>
      This leads to a good definition of <em>length</em>.
    </p>

    <fact>
      <idx><h>Dot product</h><h>and length</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Vector</h><h>length of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>length</term> of a vector <m>x</m> in <m>\R^n</m> is the number
          <md>
<mrow>\|x\| = \sqrt{x\cdot x} = \sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \cdots + x_n^2}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>

    <p>
      It is easy to see why this is true for vectors in <m>\R^2</m>, by the Pythagorean theorem.
      </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1,decoration={brace,raise=3pt}]
  \draw[grid lines] (0,0) grid (5,5);
  \point at (0,0);
  \point["$\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}$" right] (x) at (3,4);
  \draw[vector] (0,0)
    -- node[sloped,above,inner sep=1pt] {$\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5$}
    (x);
  \draw[decorate,decoration={mirror}] (0,0) -- node[below=4pt] {3} (3,0);
  \draw[decorate] (0,0) -- node[left=3pt] {4} (0,4);

  \node at (8,2.5) {$\left\|\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}\right\| = \sqrt{3^2+4^2} = 5$};

\end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
</image>
<p>
      For vectors in <m>\R^3</m>, one can check that <m>\|x\|</m> really is the length of <m>x</m>, although now this requires <em>two</em> applications of the Pythagorean theorem.
    </p>

    <p>
      Note that the length of a vector is the length of the <em>arrow</em>; if we think in terms of points, then the length is its distance from the origin.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that <m>\|x\| = 2,\;\|y\|=3,</m> and <m>x\cdot y = -4</m>.  What is <m>\|2x + 3y\|</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We compute
          <md>
<mrow>\|2x+3y\|^2 \amp= (2x+3y)\cdot(2x+3y)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 4x\cdot x + 6x\cdot y + 6x\cdot y + 9y\cdot y</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 4\|x\|^2 + 9\|y\|^2 + 12x\cdot y</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 4\cdot 4 + 9\cdot 9 - 12\cdot 4 = 49</mrow>
</md>.
          Hence <m>\|2x+3y\| = \sqrt{49} = 7.</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <fact>
      <p>
        If <m>x</m> is a vector and <m>c</m> is a scalar, then <m>\|cx\|=|c|\cdot\|x\|.</m>
      </p>
    </fact>

    <p>
      This says that scaling a vector by <m>c</m> scales its length by <m>|c|</m>.  For example,
      <md>
<mrow>\left\|\begin{pmatrix}
6 \\
8
\end{pmatrix}\right\| = \left\|2\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}\right\|
        = 2\left\|\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}\right\| = 10</mrow>
</md>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now that we have a good notion of length, we can define the <em>distance</em> between points in <m>\R^n</m>.  Recall that the difference between two points <m>x,y</m> is naturally a vector, namely, the vector <m>y-x</m> pointing from <m>x</m> to <m>y</m>.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="innerprod-distance-defn">
      <idx><h>Dot product</h><h>and distance</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Vector</h><h>distance between</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Point</h><h>distance between</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>distance</term> between two points <m>x,y</m> in <m>\R^n</m> is the length of the <xref ref="vectors-diff-pts" text="title">vector from</xref> <m>x</m> to <m>y</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>\dist(x,y) = \|y-x\|</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the distance from <m>(1,2)</m> to <m>(4,4)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m>x=(1,2)</m> and <m>y=(4,4)</m>.  Then
          <md>
<mrow>\dist(x,y) = \|y-x\| = \left\|\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}\right\| = \sqrt{3^2+2^2} = \sqrt{13}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
  \begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes]
    \draw[grid lines] (0,0) grid (5,5);
    \point["$0$" left] at (0,0);
    \point["$x$" left] (x) at (1,2);
    \point["$y$" right] (y) at (4,4);
    \draw[vector] (x) --
      node[sloped,above] {$y-x$} (y);
  \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
</image>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      Vectors with length <m>1</m> are very common in applications, so we give them a name.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="innerprod-unit-vec">
      <idx><h>Unit vector</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Vector</h><h>unit vector</h><see>Unit vector</see></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <term>unit vector</term> is a vector <m>x</m> with length <m>\|x\| = \sqrt{x\cdot x} =1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      <idx><h>Standard coordinate vectors</h><h>are unit vectors</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Unit vector</h><h>standard coordinate vectors</h></idx>
      The <xref ref="defn-standard-coord-vectors" text="title">standard coordinate vectors</xref> <m>e_1,e_2,e_3,\ldots</m> are unit vectors:
      <md>
<mrow>\|e_1\| = \left\|\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}\right\| = \sqrt{1^2 + 0^2 + 0^2} = 1</mrow>
</md>.
      For any nonzero vector <m>x</m>, there is a unique unit vector pointing in the same direction.  It is obtained by dividing by the length of <m>x</m>.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="innerprod-unit-vec-dir">
      <idx><h>Unit vector</h><h>in the direction of a vector</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Vector</h><h>unit vector in the direction of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>x</m> be a nonzero vector in <m>\R^n</m>.  The <term>unit vector in the direction of <m>x</m></term> is the vector <m>x/\|x\|</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>

    <p>
      This is in fact a unit vector (noting that <m>\|x\|</m> is a positive number, so <m>\bigl|1/\|x\|\bigr| = 1/\|x\|</m>):
      <md>
<mrow>\left\|\frac{x}{\|x\|}\right\| = \frac 1{\|x\|}\|x\| = 1</mrow>
</md>.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the unit vector <m>u</m> in the direction of <m>x = \begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We divide by the length of <m>x</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>u = \frac{x}{\|x\|} = \frac 1{\sqrt{3^2+4^2}}\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix} = \frac{1}{5}\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}
            = \begin{pmatrix}
3/5 \\
4/5
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
  \begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes]
    \draw[grid lines] (-1,-1) grid (5,5);
    \coordinate["$x$" above] (x) at (3,4);
    \draw[vector] (0,0) -- (x);
    \draw[vector,seq-red] (0,0) to["$u$"] ($1/5*(x)$);
    \point["$0$" left] at (0,0);
  \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
</image>
      </solution>
    </example>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-orthogonal-vectors">
    <title>Orthogonal Vectors</title>

    <p>
      In this section, we show how the dot product can be used to define <em>orthogonality</em>, i.e., when two vectors are perpendicular to each other.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <idx><h>Orthogonality</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Vector</h><h>orthogonal</h><see>Orthogonality</see></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two vectors <m>x,y</m> in <m>\R^n</m> are <term>orthogonal</term> or <term>perpendicular</term> if <m>x\cdot y = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      <notation><usage><m>x\perp y</m></usage><description><m>x</m> is orthogonal to <m>y</m></description></notation>
      <alert>Notation:</alert> <m>x\perp y</m> means <m>x\cdot y = 0</m>.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <idx><h>Orthogonality</h><h>zero vector</h></idx>
      <p>
        Since <m>0 \cdot x = 0</m> for any vector <m>x</m>, the zero vector is orthogonal to every vector in <m>\R^n</m>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      We motivate the above definition using the <em>law of cosines</em> in <m>\R^2</m>.  In our language, the law of cosines asserts that if <m>x,y</m> are two nonzero vectors, and if <m>\alpha>0</m> is the angle between them, then
      <md>
<mrow>\|y-x\|^2 = \|x\|^2 + \|y\|^2 - 2\|x\|\,\|y\|\cos\alpha</mrow>
</md>.
      <latex-code mode="bare">
        \usetikzlibrary{angles}
      </latex-code>
      </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=1cm]
  \point ["$x$" right] (x) at (3,1);
  \point ["$y$" above] (y) at (-2,3);
  \coordinate (o) at (0,0);
  \draw[vector] (0,0) -- node[below] {$\|x\|$} (x);
  \draw[vector] (0,0) -- node[left] {$\|y\|$} (y);
  \draw[vector] (x) -- node[sloped,above] {$\|y-x\|$} (y);
  \pic[draw, "$\alpha$"] {angle=x--o--y};
\end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
</image>
<p>
      In particular, <m>\alpha=90^\circ</m> if and only if <m>\cos(\alpha)=0</m>, which happens if and only if <m>\|y-x\|^2 = \|x\|^2+\|y\|^2</m>.  Therefore,
      <md>
<mrow>\text{$x$ and $y$ are perpendicular}
    \amp\iff \|x\|^2 + \|y\|^2 = \|y-x\|^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\iff x\cdot x + y\cdot y = (y-x)\cdot(y-x)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\iff x\cdot x + y\cdot y = y\cdot y + x\cdot x -2x\cdot y</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\iff x\cdot y = 0</mrow>
</md>.
      To reiterate:
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <idx><h>Orthogonality</h><h>and the Pythagorean theorem</h></idx>
      <p>
        <md>
<mrow>x\perp y \quad\iff\quad x\cdot y = 0 \quad\iff\quad \|y-x\|^2 = \|x\|^2 + \|y\|^2</mrow>
</md>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>Find all vectors orthogonal to <m>v = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix}.</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have to find all vectors <m>x</m> such that <m>x\cdot v = 0</m>.  This means solving the equation
          <md>
<mrow>0 = x\cdot v = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix} = x_1 + x_2 - x_3</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form for the solution set is <m>x_1 = -x_2 + x_3</m>, so the parametric vector form of the general solution is
          <md>
<mrow>x = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix} = x_2\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} + x_3\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Therefore, the answer is the <em>plane</em>
          <md>
<mrow>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
          For instance,
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}\perp\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix} \sptxt{because} \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix} = 0</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>Find all vectors orthogonal to both <m>v = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>w = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have to solve the system of two homogeneous equations
          <md>
<mrow>\left.\begin{alignedat}{2}
0 \amp {}={} \amp x\cdot v = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix} = x_1 + x_2 - x_3 \amp {} \\
0 \amp {}={} \amp x\cdot w = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}  = x_1 + x_2 + x_3 \amp .
\end{alignedat}\right</mrow>
</md>.
          In matrix form:
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric vector form of the solution set is
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix} = x_2\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Therefore, the answer is the <em>line</em>
          <md>
<mrow>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
          For instance,
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix} = 0 \sptxt{and}
            \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} = 0</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark>
      <title>Angle between two vectors</title>
      <idx><h>Vector</h><h>angle between</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Dot product</h><h>and angles</h></idx>
      <p>
        More generally, the law of cosines gives a formula for the angle <m>\alpha</m> between two nonzero vectors:
        <md>
<mrow>2\|x\|\|y\|\cos(\alpha)
          \amp= \|x\|^2 + \|y\|^2 - \|y-x\|^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= x\cdot x + y\cdot y - (y-x)\cdot (y-x)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= x\cdot x + y\cdot y - y\cdot y - x\cdot x + 2x\cdot y</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 2x\cdot y</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\quad\implies \alpha = \cos\inv\left(\frac{x\cdot y}{\|x\|\|y\|}\right)</mrow>
</md>.
        In higher dimensions, we take this to be the <em>definition</em> of the angle between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
      </p>
    </remark>

  </subsection>

<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Compute the dot product of the vectors <m>\begin{pmatrix}-3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and
      <m>\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 2 \\ -4 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p><m>\begin{pmatrix}-3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 2 \\ -4 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}
        = -3(0) + 1(2) + 2(-4) + 1(3) = -3</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are vectors satisfying <m>||u|| = 3</m>,
        <m>||v|| = 9</m>, and <m>u \cdot v = -4</m>.  Find
        <md><mrow>(4u-v) \cdot (u + v)</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>(4u-v) \cdot (u + v)
            \amp  = 4 u \cdot u + 4u \cdot v - v \dot u - v \dot v</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = 4 ||u||^2 + 4 (-4) - (-4) - ||v||^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = 4 (3^2) - 16 + 4 - 9^2
              = -57</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are vectors satisfying <m>||u|| = 4</m>
        and <m>||v|| = 2</m>.  Find
        <md><mrow>||u+v||^2 + ||u-v||^2</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We could expand the expression or use the Parallelogram Law.
        <md><mrow>||u+v||^2 + ||u-v||^2 = 2(||u||^2 + ||v||^2)
            = 2(16 + 4) = 40</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>u</m>, <m>v</m>, and <m>w</m> are vectors with the property that
      <m>u \perp v</m> and <m>u \perp w</m>.  Must it be true that <m>u</m> is orthogonal
      to all vectors in <m>\textup{Span}\{v, w\}</m>?  Justify your answer.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        (Challenge!) We can define dot products outside of our usual
        context of <m>{\bf R}^n</m>.  This example discusses a dot product on the
        set <m>W</m> of all polynomials of degree <m>3</m> or less, defined as follows.
        If <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are in <m>W</m>, we define a dot product using integration:
        <md><mrow>f \cdot g = \int_{-1} ^1 f(x) g(x) \ dx</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>f(x) = x</m> and <m>g(x) = x^2</m>.  Show that <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>
          are orthogonal.  In other words, show that <m>f \cdot g = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p><m>\displaystyle f \cdot g = \int_{-1}^1 x(x^2) \ dx = \int_{-1}^1
            x^3 \ dx = x^4 \Big|_{-1}^1 = 1 - 1 = 0</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>f(x) = x</m> and <m>g(x) = x^3 - cx</m>.  Find the value
          of <m>c</m> so that <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are orthogonal.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We solve for <m>c</m> so that <m>f \cdot g = 0</m>.
          <md><mrow>0 = f \cdot g = \int_{-1}^1 x(x^3 - cx) \ dx
                = \int_{-1}^1 x^4 - cx^2
                = \Big[\dfrac{x^5}{5} - \dfrac{1}{3}cx^3\Big]_{-1}^1
                = \dfrac{2}{5} - \dfrac{2}{3}c</mrow></md>.
          Therefore, <m>c = \dfrac{3}{5}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</exercises>
</section>
